-- D2's interaction vocabulary: the causal identity, the timed outcome, and the
-- single recorded interaction.
--
-- WI-A13, stage 2. These types were declared by stage 1 inside
-- `src/core/dst_program.ail`. They are moved here unchanged, for one structural
-- reason: stage 2 records interactions AT THE PORT SEAM, so `WorldState` in
-- `src/core/ports.ail` has to carry a list of them, and `dst_program` cannot be
-- imported from there — it names `ExecutionManifest`, which drags the whole
-- 1559-line `dst_profile` closure into the production driver's import graph.
--
-- The alternative was a second, flatter "recorded request" record declared in
-- `ports.ail` and projected into `Interaction` by the discovery module. That is
-- rejected deliberately: two representations of one fact with a hand-written
-- projection between them is the exact shape of the fifteen silent defects this
-- project has now found, and the projection would be written by the same code
-- whose completeness is under test. One representation, moved down.
--
-- This module therefore imports NOTHING but std. That is a load-bearing
-- property, not an accident: it is what lets both the production port layer and
-- the DST program layer name the same `Interaction`.
--
-- The three design decisions stage 1 recorded for these types are unchanged and
-- restated here, because this is now where a reader finds them.
--
-- 1. CAUSAL IDENTITY IS A SUM, NOT A FLAT RECORD. D2 specifies different
--    identity fields per class: provider adds step and model, tool and approval
--    add call id and name, an extension effect adds the effect class id. A flat
--    record holding all of them makes "a provider identity carrying a call id"
--    representable, and then the validator has to forbid what the type should
--    never have allowed. The `kind` string is DERIVED from the body
--    (`identity_kind`), so a kind that disagrees with its own payload cannot be
--    written down.
--
-- 2. THE ORDINAL IS WHAT "DUPLICATE IDENTITY" MEANS. D2 asks the validator to
--    reject duplicate interaction identities, and in the same paragraph asks
--    that repeated production call ids stay REPRESENTABLE — "so an invariant
--    can reject them as system behavior rather than the program decoder
--    rejecting the artifact". Those two only fit together one way: the decoder
--    rejects a repeated ENCOUNTER ORDINAL, which is a malformed artifact, and
--    says nothing about a repeated call id, which is a finding about the system
--    under test.
--
-- 3. `deadline_ms` IS -1 FOR THE CLASSES D2 GIVES NO DEADLINE, rather than an
--    Option, because the value is compared, ordered and encoded on every path.

module src/core/dst_interaction

import std/list as List (length)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Causal identity (D2)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The per-class identity payload. See design note 1 in the module header.
--
-- `ExtensionEffectIdentity`'s call id is "" where the effect has none, which is
-- D2's "call id where the effect has one" — the only place a blank component is
-- legitimate rather than a missing required value.
export type IdentityBody
  = ProviderIdentity(string, int, string)           -- origin, step, model
  | ToolIdentity(string, string, string)            -- origin, call_id, name
  | ApprovalIdentity(string, string, string)        -- origin, call_id, name
  | ExtensionEffectIdentity(string, string, string) -- ext_id, class_id, call_id
  | EnvironmentReadIdentity(string)                 -- key
  | RandomDrawIdentity(string)                      -- bounds label
  | ClockAdvanceIdentity
  -- WI-D17's filesystem mutators. ONE component, the path, and NO origin — the
  -- `EnvironmentReadIdentity` shape rather than the `ToolIdentity` shape,
  -- because these are the DRIVER's own seams and D2 gives an origin only to the
  -- three classes that answer a named producer's request.
  --
  -- WHAT THAT COSTS AND WHO PAYS IT, stated rather than discovered at part 2:
  -- when compose's writes are routed, the producer becomes an EXTENSION and
  -- "which extension wrote this path" stops being derivable from the log.
  -- Adding the component later is a constructor arity change, which reaches
  -- `identity_kind`, `identity_origin`, `identity_body_eq`,
  -- `identity_body_projection`, `dst_program.validate_identity`,
  -- `dst_secrets.scan_identity`, `dst_persistence.identity_slots`/`body_of` and
  -- both directions of `ext_world`'s JSON — nine sites. It is NOT taken here
  -- because a component that is blank at every construction site in the tree is
  -- a field no fixture can distinguish from an absent one, and D2's own rule for
  -- `ExtensionEffectIdentity` is that a blank component is legitimate only where
  -- the class genuinely has none.
  | FileWriteIdentity(string)                       -- path
  | FileRemoveIdentity(string)                      -- path
  -- WI-D18's directory create, on the same shape and for the same reason. The
  -- two READS the directory class adds (`path_stat`, `dir_list`) get no
  -- constructor at all, and that is WI-D17 §3's line rather than a new
  -- judgement: the log records the driver's ACTS, and reconstitutes the world's
  -- SUPPLY. A read of a table `InitialWorld` cannot carry is neither.
  | DirMakeIdentity(string)                         -- path

-- The stable kind id, DERIVED. These strings appear in the interaction log and
-- in harness-failure projections, so they are part of the artifact surface and
-- change only with a schema version.
--
-- WI-D17 ADDED TWO AND MOVED NO VERSION, and the reason is worth stating
-- because "part of the artifact surface" reads as though it should have.
-- Measured across the three versions that could plausibly have been the one:
--
--   `event_vocabulary_version` ("event-vocabulary/1") versions the 34
--     `LedgerEvent` variants and their wire projection. It has nothing to do
--     with this sum; naming it here would be versioning the wrong artifact.
--   `program_schema_version` ("execution-program/1") versions the persisted
--     program's SHAPE. Adding a constructor adds no field and removes none, and
--     no existing program's bytes change meaning.
--   The persistence codec's own tag vocabulary. `dst_persistence.body_of`
--     REFUSES an unknown kind (`UnknownInteractionKind`), so an old build handed
--     a new artifact rejects it loudly rather than reinterpreting it — which is
--     precisely the behaviour D8's rule demands a version bump in order to
--     guarantee, already guaranteed structurally.
--
-- ONE DECODER IS NOT LOUD, AND IT IS REPORTED RATHER THAN FIXED HERE:
-- `ext_world.identity_body_of` falls through to `ClockAdvanceIdentity` for an
-- unrecognised tag, by its own stated design ("a total decoder is the right
-- shape"). An old build reading a new `file_write` interaction over that
-- transport therefore reads a clock advance. That was already true for every
-- constructor this sum has ever gained; it is named here because this item is
-- the first to gain one since the note was written, and closing it is a change
-- to a total decoder's contract, not to this list.
export pure func identity_kind(b: IdentityBody) -> string {
  match b {
    ProviderIdentity(_, _, _) => "expect_provider",
    ToolIdentity(_, _, _) => "expect_tool",
    ApprovalIdentity(_, _, _) => "expect_approval",
    ExtensionEffectIdentity(_, _, _) => "expect_extension_effect",
    EnvironmentReadIdentity(_) => "environment_read",
    RandomDrawIdentity(_) => "runtime_random_draw",
    ClockAdvanceIdentity => "advance_clock",
    FileWriteIdentity(_) => "file_write",
    FileRemoveIdentity(_) => "file_remove",
    DirMakeIdentity(_) => "dir_make"
  }
}

-- Every kind id this vocabulary defines, in a fixed order. Stage 2's
-- per-class completeness report iterates THIS rather than a list written out at
-- the assertion site: a class added to `IdentityBody` and forgotten at the
-- assertion is a class whose recording nobody checks.
export pure func all_interaction_kinds() -> [string] {
  [identity_kind(ProviderIdentity("", 0, "")),
   identity_kind(ToolIdentity("", "", "")),
   identity_kind(ApprovalIdentity("", "", "")),
   identity_kind(ExtensionEffectIdentity("", "", "")),
   identity_kind(EnvironmentReadIdentity("")),
   identity_kind(RandomDrawIdentity("")),
   identity_kind(ClockAdvanceIdentity),
   identity_kind(FileWriteIdentity("")),
   identity_kind(FileRemoveIdentity("")),
   identity_kind(DirMakeIdentity(""))]
}

-- The origin, derived the same way. Blank for the three classes D2 gives no
-- origin: an environment read, a random draw, and a clock advance are world
-- events, not requests from a named producer.
export pure func identity_origin(b: IdentityBody) -> string {
  match b {
    ProviderIdentity(o, _, _) => o,
    ToolIdentity(o, _, _) => o,
    ApprovalIdentity(o, _, _) => o,
    ExtensionEffectIdentity(e, _, _) => e,
    EnvironmentReadIdentity(_) => "",
    RandomDrawIdentity(_) => "",
    ClockAdvanceIdentity => "",
    FileWriteIdentity(_) => "",
    FileRemoveIdentity(_) => "",
    DirMakeIdentity(_) => ""
  }
}

-- Whether two identity bodies are the same logical request. Used by strict
-- replay; defined here because it is a property of the identity, not of the
-- replay mode.
export pure func identity_body_eq(a: IdentityBody, b: IdentityBody) -> bool {
  match a {
    ProviderIdentity(o, s, m) =>
      match b { ProviderIdentity(o2, s2, m2) => o == o2 && s == s2 && m == m2, _ => false },
    ToolIdentity(o, c, n) =>
      match b { ToolIdentity(o2, c2, n2) => o == o2 && c == c2 && n == n2, _ => false },
    ApprovalIdentity(o, c, n) =>
      match b { ApprovalIdentity(o2, c2, n2) => o == o2 && c == c2 && n == n2, _ => false },
    ExtensionEffectIdentity(e, k, c) =>
      match b { ExtensionEffectIdentity(e2, k2, c2) => e == e2 && k == k2 && c == c2, _ => false },
    EnvironmentReadIdentity(k) =>
      match b { EnvironmentReadIdentity(k2) => k == k2, _ => false },
    RandomDrawIdentity(l) =>
      match b { RandomDrawIdentity(l2) => l == l2, _ => false },
    ClockAdvanceIdentity =>
      match b { ClockAdvanceIdentity => true, _ => false },
    FileWriteIdentity(p) =>
      match b { FileWriteIdentity(p2) => p == p2, _ => false },
    FileRemoveIdentity(p) =>
      match b { FileRemoveIdentity(p2) => p == p2, _ => false },
    DirMakeIdentity(p) =>
      match b { DirMakeIdentity(p2) => p == p2, _ => false }
  }
}

-- A human- and diff-readable projection of the body. This is what a
-- HarnessFailure's `actual_request_projection` carries, so it must be total and
-- must not lose a component.
export pure func identity_body_projection(b: IdentityBody) -> string {
  match b {
    ProviderIdentity(o, s, m) => "provider origin=${o} step=${s} model=${m}",
    ToolIdentity(o, c, n) => "tool origin=${o} call_id=${c} name=${n}",
    ApprovalIdentity(o, c, n) => "approval origin=${o} call_id=${c} name=${n}",
    ExtensionEffectIdentity(e, k, c) => "extension_effect ext=${e} class=${k} call_id=${c}",
    EnvironmentReadIdentity(k) => "env_read key=${k}",
    RandomDrawIdentity(l) => "random_draw bounds=${l}",
    ClockAdvanceIdentity => "advance_clock",
    FileWriteIdentity(p) => "file_write path=${p}",
    FileRemoveIdentity(p) => "file_remove path=${p}",
    DirMakeIdentity(p) => "dir_make path=${p}"
  }
}

-- D2: "Every interaction identity includes a global encounter ordinal."
export type CausalIdentity = {
  ordinal: int,
  body: IdentityBody
}

export pure func identity_projection(i: CausalIdentity) -> string {
  "#${i.ordinal} ${identity_body_projection(i.body)}"
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Outcomes (D2, D4)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- A recorded stream chunk at its virtual offset. D2: "ordered stream chunks
-- with non-decreasing virtual offsets followed by a final StepResult or
-- AIError".
export type StreamChunkRecord = {
  offset_ms: int,
  text: string
}

-- `OutcomeMissing` is D2's `value_or_missing` for an environment read. It is a
-- third status rather than an error because a key that is absent is a NORMAL
-- read whose answer is "unset" — the driver's `getEnvOr` fallback path — and
-- classifying it as a fault would manufacture a fault class that the catalogue
-- does not contain.
export type OutcomeStatus = OutcomeOk | OutcomeFault | OutcomeMissing

export pure func outcome_status_id(s: OutcomeStatus) -> string {
  match s {
    OutcomeOk => "ok",
    OutcomeFault => "fault",
    OutcomeMissing => "missing"
  }
}

-- D2's `timed_outcome`: "deterministic latency or completion time. Before
-- delivery, the world advances its monotonic clock by the recorded non-negative
-- amount."
--
-- `fault_class_id` is one of A7's stable class ids, and is blank exactly when
-- the status is not `OutcomeFault`.
export type TimedOutcome = {
  advance_ms: int,
  chunks: [StreamChunkRecord],
  payload: string,
  status: OutcomeStatus,
  fault_class_id: string
}

export pure func ok_outcome(advance_ms: int, payload: string) -> TimedOutcome {
  { advance_ms: advance_ms, chunks: [], payload: payload, status: OutcomeOk, fault_class_id: "" }
}

export pure func fault_outcome(advance_ms: int, payload: string, class_id: string) -> TimedOutcome {
  { advance_ms: advance_ms, chunks: [], payload: payload, status: OutcomeFault, fault_class_id: class_id }
}

export pure func missing_outcome() -> TimedOutcome {
  { advance_ms: 0, chunks: [], payload: "", status: OutcomeMissing, fault_class_id: "" }
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Interactions
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- D2: the resolved interactions are "an ordered expected protocol, not
-- anonymous response queues", each recording a stable causal identity AND a
-- bounded full request projection used for strict reproduction.
export type Interaction = {
  identity: CausalIdentity,
  request_projection: string,
  deadline_ms: int,
  outcome: TimedOutcome
}

export pure func interaction_kind(i: Interaction) -> string {
  identity_kind(i.identity.body)
}

-- Whether this class carries a deadline at all (D2's grammar: ExpectTool,
-- ExpectApproval and ExpectExtensionEffect do; the rest do not).
export pure func class_has_deadline(b: IdentityBody) -> bool {
  match b {
    ToolIdentity(_, _, _) => true,
    ApprovalIdentity(_, _, _) => true,
    ExtensionEffectIdentity(_, _, _) => true,
    ProviderIdentity(_, _, _) => false,
    EnvironmentReadIdentity(_) => false,
    RandomDrawIdentity(_) => false,
    ClockAdvanceIdentity => false,
    -- A filesystem mutation is synchronous and has no timeout at the seam:
    -- `Ports.file_write` takes no `timeout_ms` the way `ToolInvocation` does, so
    -- recording a deadline here would be inventing one. `recording_file_write`
    -- records -1, which is what this false means.
    FileWriteIdentity(_) => false,
    FileRemoveIdentity(_) => false,
    DirMakeIdentity(_) => false
  }
}

-- Only a provider outcome may carry stream chunks. D2 puts chunks on the
-- provider timed outcome specifically, and D9 keeps chunk callbacks serial
-- within the provider request rather than making them independent actors.
export pure func class_may_stream(b: IdentityBody) -> bool {
  match b {
    ProviderIdentity(_, _, _) => true,
    _ => false
  }
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Log queries. Stage 2's assertions are counts and identity comparisons over a
-- recorded log, and they are declared HERE rather than at the assertion site so
-- that the recorder and the checker cannot drift into two notions of "how many
-- of this class are in the log".
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

export pure func count_kind(xs: [Interaction], kind: string) -> int {
  match xs {
    [] => 0,
    i :: rest => (if interaction_kind(i) == kind then 1 else 0) + count_kind(rest, kind)
  }
}

export pure func of_kind(xs: [Interaction], kind: string) -> [Interaction] {
  match xs {
    [] => [],
    i :: rest =>
      if interaction_kind(i) == kind then i :: of_kind(rest, kind) else of_kind(rest, kind)
  }
}

-- WI-D4. How many interactions in this log the GENERATOR authored, as opposed
-- to how many the log holds.
--
-- THE DIFFERENCE IS THE DEFECT WI-D4 EXISTS TO REMOVE. `max_interactions` is a
-- bound on a generator schema (D2: "Each GENERATOR schema declares hard bounds
-- for interactions"), and until this function existed both the bound's
-- enforcement and the request salts read `List.length(state.log)` instead —
-- the whole log, including every environment read the DRIVER made for its own
-- configuration. Two consequences, and the second is the one that hid:
--
--   * the driver's reads SPENT the generator's budget, truncating trajectories;
--   * the driver's reads entered the SALT, so they changed which branch every
--     generated choice took. A generated trajectory was therefore a function of
--     how many times the driver happened to read its config, and WI-D3 changing
--     that count silently reshuffled every seed in the fixed bank — which is how
--     `ToolCorrelationMismatch` stopped being reached by the two seeds pinned as
--     its witnesses.
--
-- The three kinds counted are the three the GENERATING adapters serve: a
-- provider exchange, a tool execution and an approval read are each a request
-- the generator answers by CHOOSING. An environment read is answered from a map
-- chosen up front, a clock advance is read back from the seam that caused it,
-- and an extension effect comes from an installed hook — none of the three is a
-- generator choice, and none of them may move one.
--
-- DERIVED from `identity_kind` rather than written as three string literals, for
-- the reason the header of this section gives: a recorder and a checker with two
-- notions of "how many of this class" is the drift this module exists to
-- prevent.
export pure func generator_authored_count(xs: [Interaction]) -> int {
  count_kind(xs, identity_kind(ProviderIdentity("", 0, "")))
    + count_kind(xs, identity_kind(ToolIdentity("", "", "")))
    + count_kind(xs, identity_kind(ApprovalIdentity("", "", "")))
}

-- The sum of every recorded clock advance. This is the quantity the world's own
-- `clock_ms` delta is compared against — the cross-check the handoff calls
-- general across classes, because EVERY class may advance the clock.
export pure func total_advance_ms(xs: [Interaction]) -> int {
  match xs {
    [] => 0,
    i :: rest => i.outcome.advance_ms + total_advance_ms(rest)
  }
}

-- How many recorded approval reads actually CONSUMED an answer.
--
-- An approval read that finds the queue empty returns end-of-input and advances
-- nothing (`scripted_approval`), so it is recorded with an `OutcomeMissing`
-- status; one that is served consumes exactly one entry. The world's own
-- `approvals` cursor delta must therefore equal this count EXACTLY — which
-- turns the cursor from a proxy for "how many reads happened" into an exact
-- statement about which ones were served.
--
-- The distinction is not academic. The cursor delta was used directly as the
-- approval witness in stage 2's first version, and it UNDER-COUNTS the moment a
-- queue runs dry: three reads against a one-entry queue move the cursor by one
-- while the driver made three requests, so a CORRECT recorder was reported as
-- over-recording. See `dst_discovery`'s witness comments.
export pure func approvals_served(xs: [Interaction]) -> int {
  match xs {
    [] => 0,
    i :: rest =>
      (match i.identity.body {
         ApprovalIdentity(_, _, _) =>
           match i.outcome.status { OutcomeMissing => 0, _ => 1 },
         _ => 0
       }) + approvals_served(rest)
  }
}

-- The env keys the log claims were read, in encounter order and with repeats
-- preserved. Repeats matter: the driver reads `MOTOKO_TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS` once per
-- native tool dispatch, so collapsing them would hide a recorder that logs the
-- first read of a key and drops the rest.
export pure func env_keys_read(xs: [Interaction]) -> [string] {
  match xs {
    [] => [],
    i :: rest =>
      match i.identity.body {
        EnvironmentReadIdentity(k) => k :: env_keys_read(rest),
        _ => env_keys_read(rest)
      }
  }
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Inline tests: the derivations on the identity itself, which have no fixture.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pure func contains_str(xs: [string], target: string) -> bool {
  match xs {
    [] => false,
    x :: rest => if x == target then true else contains_str(rest, target)
  }
}

pure func all_distinct(xs: [string], seen: [string]) -> bool {
  match xs {
    [] => true,
    x :: rest => if contains_str(seen, x) then false else all_distinct(rest, seen ++ [x])
  }
}

-- The kind is derived from the body, so there is no representable program in
-- which a kind disagrees with its own payload (design note 1). This asserts the
-- derivation is TOTAL and INJECTIVE across the ten classes — if two classes
-- ever derived the same kind string, the interaction log would conflate them
-- and the completeness count per class would silently merge two classes.
--
-- WI-D17 took it from seven to nine, and the injectivity clause is what made the
-- cheap design fail early: a single `FileMutationIdentity(op, path)` serving both
-- mutators would have to derive its kind from `op`, which makes
-- `FileMutationIdentity("bogus", p)` representable and reading as a write. Two
-- constructors is what design note 1 asks for and this is the assertion that
-- says so.
--
-- WI-D18 took it to ten with `DirMakeIdentity`, and the count is what says the
-- directory class did NOT reuse a mutator's constructor. `FileWriteIdentity` for
-- a `mkdirAll` would have type-checked at every one of the nine sites this sum
-- reaches, produced a log whose every count still balanced, and recorded a
-- directory create as a file write of zero bytes — a kind disagreeing with its
-- own payload, which is what design note 1 forbids and what this row measures.
pure func test_identity_kind_is_injective_across_the_ten_classes() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let ks = all_interaction_kinds();
    List.length(ks) == 10 && all_distinct(ks, [])
  }

-- Identity equality must discriminate on EVERY component D2 names, in both
-- directions. A comparison that ignored, say, the provider's step would let
-- strict replay deliver step 2's outcome to step 1's request and still call
-- itself a reproduction.
pure func test_identity_equality_discriminates_every_component() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    identity_body_eq(ProviderIdentity("o", 1, "m"), ProviderIdentity("o", 1, "m"))
      && not identity_body_eq(ProviderIdentity("o", 1, "m"), ProviderIdentity("x", 1, "m"))
      && not identity_body_eq(ProviderIdentity("o", 1, "m"), ProviderIdentity("o", 2, "m"))
      && not identity_body_eq(ProviderIdentity("o", 1, "m"), ProviderIdentity("o", 1, "x"))
      && not identity_body_eq(ToolIdentity("o", "c", "n"), ToolIdentity("o", "c", "x"))
      && not identity_body_eq(ToolIdentity("o", "c", "n"), ToolIdentity("o", "x", "n"))
      && not identity_body_eq(ExtensionEffectIdentity("e", "k", "c"), ExtensionEffectIdentity("e", "x", "c"))
      && not identity_body_eq(EnvironmentReadIdentity("a"), EnvironmentReadIdentity("b"))
      && not identity_body_eq(RandomDrawIdentity("a"), RandomDrawIdentity("b"))
      -- WI-D17. The path discriminates, and — the half a one-constructor design
      -- would have lost — a write and a remove of the SAME path are different
      -- logical requests. Strict replay delivering one to the other would be a
      -- reproduction that deleted what the run created.
      && not identity_body_eq(FileWriteIdentity("a"), FileWriteIdentity("b"))
      && not identity_body_eq(FileRemoveIdentity("a"), FileRemoveIdentity("b"))
      && not identity_body_eq(FileWriteIdentity("a"), FileRemoveIdentity("a"))
      && identity_body_eq(FileWriteIdentity("a"), FileWriteIdentity("a"))
      -- WI-D18. A directory create and a file write AT THE SAME PATH are
      -- different logical requests, and that pair is the one worth asserting:
      -- they are the two acts a routed compose performs one line apart
      -- (`mkdirAll` then `writeFile`), so a replay that delivered one to the
      -- other would be a reproduction that created a directory where the run
      -- wrote a file.
      && not identity_body_eq(DirMakeIdentity("a"), DirMakeIdentity("b"))
      && not identity_body_eq(DirMakeIdentity("a"), FileWriteIdentity("a"))
      && not identity_body_eq(DirMakeIdentity("a"), FileRemoveIdentity("a"))
      && identity_body_eq(DirMakeIdentity("a"), DirMakeIdentity("a"))
  }

-- A tool and an approval sharing origin, call id and name are DIFFERENT logical
-- requests. They are structurally distinct constructors, so this cannot regress
-- silently — but the driver genuinely issues both against one call id, so the
-- claim is worth stating where a reader of the replay code will find it.
pure func test_tool_and_approval_on_one_call_id_are_distinct() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    not identity_body_eq(ToolIdentity("loop_v2", "call_1", "BashExec"),
                         ApprovalIdentity("loop_v2", "call_1", "BashExec"))
  }

-- `count_kind` must discriminate, not merely count. A recorder that logs every
-- class under one kind string would satisfy a total count and fail this.
pure func test_count_kind_discriminates_classes() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let mk = func(b: IdentityBody, ord: int) -> Interaction {
      { identity: { ordinal: ord, body: b },
        request_projection: "p",
        deadline_ms: if class_has_deadline(b) then 0 else -1,
        outcome: ok_outcome(0, "") }
    };
    let xs = [mk(ProviderIdentity("d", 0, "m"), 0),
              mk(ClockAdvanceIdentity, 1),
              mk(ProviderIdentity("d", 1, "m"), 2)];
    count_kind(xs, "expect_provider") == 2
      && count_kind(xs, "advance_clock") == 1
      && count_kind(xs, "expect_tool") == 0
      && total_advance_ms(xs) == 0
  }
